w: 1.~Lord, from the ill and fro- ward man give me de- li- ve- rance, And do thou safe pre- serve me from the man of vi- o- lence:
w: * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
w: 3.~Much like un- to a ser- pent's tongue their tongues they sharp do make; And un- der- neath their lips there lies the poi- son of a snake.
w: 4.~Lord, keep me from the wick- ed's hands, from vi'- lent men me save; Who ut- ter- ly to ov- er- throw my go- ings pur- posed have.
w: 5.~The proud for me a snare have hid, and cords; yea, they a net Have by the wa- y\-side for me spread; they gins for me have set.
w: 6.~I said un- to the Lord, Thou art my God: un- to the cry O- f all my sup- pli- ca- tions, Lord, do thine ear ap- ply.
w: 7.~O God the Lord, who art the strength of my sal- va- ti- on: A co- v'ring in the day of war my head thou hast put on.
w: 8.~Un- to the wi- cked man, O Lord, his wi- shes do not grant; Nor fur- ther thou his ill de- vice, lest they them- selves should vaunt.
w: 9.~As for the head and chief of those a- bout that com- pass me, Ev'n by the mis- chief of their lips let thou them co- ver'd be.
w: 10.~Let burn- ing coals up- on them fall, them throw in fie- ry flame, And in deep pits, that they no more may rise out of the same.
w: 11.~Let not an e- vil spea- ker be on earth e- sta- blish- èd: Mis- chief shall hunt the vi'- lent man, till he be ru- in- èd.
w: 12.~I know God will th'af- flic- ted's cause main- tain, and poor men's right. Sure- ly the just shall praise thy name; th'u- pright dwell in thy sight.
